* {{audio|en|En-us-culminate.ogg|Audio (US)}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive|astronomy}} Of a [[heavenly body]], to be at the highest point, reach its greatest altitude.
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[reach]] the (physical or figurative) [[summit]], highest point, [[peak]] etc.
#: {{syn|en|peak}}
#* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|3|passage=As when his beams at noon / '''Culminate''' from the equator.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1875|author=James Dwight Dana|title=Manual of Geology
|passage=The type of Cycads '''culminated''' in the Mesozoic}}
#* {{RQ:Motley Dutch Republic|passage=The house of Burgundy was rapidly '''culminating'''.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=2019|month=October|author=Tony Miles; Philip Sherratt|title=EMR kicks off new era|journal=Modern Railways|page=53|text=This '''culminates''' in a timetable change in December 2020, at which point EMR will introduce a sixth train each hour out of St Pancras.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|figuratively}} To reach a [[climax]]; to come to a decisive point, especially an end or conclusion.
#: {{ux|en|Their messy breakup '''culminated''' in a restraining order.}}
#: {{ux|en|The class will '''culminate''' with a rigorous examination.}}
#* {{quote-web|en|date=2006-09-12|title=President Bush’s Reality| work=New York Times| url=http://www.nytimes.com/2006/09/12/opinion/12tue1.html| passage=Mr. Bush has been marking the fifth anniversary of Sept. 11 with a series of speeches about terrorism that '''culminated''' with his televised address last night.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|url=https://archive.org/details/isbn_9781843535270/|title = The Rough Guide to Taiwan|series=w:Rough Guides|author = Stephen Keeling; Brice Minnigh|publisher = Penguin|year = 2007|isbn = 978-1-84353-527-0|OCLC=466340762|location = |page =510|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/isbn_9781843535270/page/510/|text=Born in 1866 in Pingdong County in the south of the island, Lin became an outlaw after a brief stint as a Qing dynasty official, but after the Japanese invasion in 1895 he used his bandit experience to organize guerrilla resistance. This '''culminated''' in successful attacks on Donggang in 1897, and Chaojhou in 1898 with an army of two thousand that included Paiwan and Hakka troops as well as Taiwanese.}}
# {{lb|en|US|military|of an offensive, etc}} To reach a point at which continued progress is not possible<!-- though the attacker may nonetheless keep pushing but achieving nothing-->.
#* {{quote-book|en|date=2014-08-15|author=Major Kevin R. Kilbride|title=Military Assistance Advisory Group-Vietnam (1954-1963): The Battle Of Ap Bac|publisher=Pickle Partners Publishing|isbn=9781782896906
|text=Approaching from the south on foot, elements of the CG battalion were first to come into contact and their attack '''culminated''' because the rate and accuracy of the VC small arms fire. The CG battalions attempted two assaults to penetrate{{nb...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|date=2014-08-15|author=Major Bryan L. Woodcock|title=91st Infantry In World War I--Analysis Of An AEF Division's Efforts To Achieve Battlefield Success [Illustrated Edition]|publisher=Pickle Partners Publishing|isbn=9781782895954|page=33
|text=As a result, the attack '''culminated''' the first day without additional artillery support. The towns of Eclisfontaine and Epinonville were not held on the first day as originally directed in the Corps plan.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[finalize]], bring to a [[conclusion]], form the climax of.
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=26 Apr 86|journal=Gay Community News|page=18|title=Personal advertisement|author=Tony Marcus Antuan Haywood|text=I would like to know if there are any true moralists who would like to correspond with someone who just instinctively feels there's something wrong somewhere in this unbenevolent world and wants to save it by '''culminating''' love and eradicating the captive emotions of the self (Ego).}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=By the skin of her teeth|journal=The Economist|date=7 Sep 2010
|passage=The announcement by Tony Windsor and Rob Oakeshott in Canberra '''culminated''' more than a fortnight of intensive political horse-trading.}}

